SISSIz - Dinucleotide controlled random alignments and RNA gene prediction
==========================================================================


1. DESCRIPTION
==============


SISSIz randomizes multiple sequence alignments preserving the average
dinucleotide content. It uses a simulation procedure guided by a
phylogenetic tree.

In combination with the RNAalifold algorithm it can be used to detect
functional RNA structures in multiple alignments. To this end, SISSIz
calculates the consensus folding energy of the original data and
compares it to the expected energy distribution of the random
alignments. The significance of the prediction is measures as a
z-score:

    (Native energy) - (Mean random energy)
z = --------------------------------------
         Standard dev. random energy

Negative z-scores indicate secondary structures that are more
conserved/stable than expected by chance. 

Version 0.2.0 corrects several bugs that change the numbers SISSIz
reports, pairwise alignments most of all. Results from earlier versions
are not comparable and should be regenerated. See ChangeLog.md.


2. INSTALLATION
===============

This packages uses the GNU configuration and installation
system and thus compilation and installation should be easy:

cd /somedir/SISSIz-VERSION
./configure
make
make install (as root)

This installs the SISSIz binary into /usr/local/bin and additional
data files and examples in /usr/local/share/SISSIz.

If you have no root rights on your system or prefer to install SISSIz
into a self-contained directory run configure for example like this:

./configure --prefix=/opt/programs/SISSIz --datadir=/opt/programs/SISSIz/share


2.1 Testing the build
---------------------

    make check

Functional tests, about ten seconds. Checks that the sampled alignments
reproduce the identity and nucleotide composition of the input, that
--seed makes a run reproducible, that stdout carries the result line and
nothing else, that unusable input is refused, and that out of range
options are rejected.

    make check-asan

Rebuilds in a scratch directory under AddressSanitizer and
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and runs the suite there, plus a sweep of
every fixture through every mode. The configured tree is left alone.

    make check-valgrind

Runs the shipped alignments through valgrind in every mode and fails on
any leak or memory error not listed in tests/valgrind.supp.

    make check-stats

Draws several hundred alignments from SISSIz's own null model, scores
them, and checks that the z-scores are centred on zero. An offset here
means a shifted false positive rate in a screen. Takes a few minutes;
NULLDIST_REPLICATES trades runtime against resolution.

All of this runs on GitHub Actions for every push and pull request
(.github/workflows/ci.yml): the functional suite on Linux and macOS with
gcc and clang, a sanitizer build, and make distcheck. The statistical
check runs weekly and on demand.


3. USAGE
========

3.1 Input alignment
-------------------

The input alignment must be in CLUSTAL W format or MAF format, hold at
least two sequences, and all sequences must be of the same length.

Characters other than A, C, G, T and U are left out when the mono- and
dinucleotide content is estimated; SISSIz reports on stderr how many
were dropped. Gaps are not simulated. They are taken from the input and
put back into the same columns of every sampled alignment, so the gap
pattern of the input is preserved exactly.


3.2 Program call
----------------

SISSIz [OPTIONS] [FILE]

3.3 Options
-----------

Most options have a long (--long) and a short form (-x).

-s, --simulate

This switchtes to "simulation only" mode, i.e no RNA analysis is
carried out and the alignments is just randomized.

-n, --num-samples

Number of samples that are used for calculating the z-score. In
simulation mode, the number of random alignments to be
produced. Default is 100 for z-scores, and 1 for simulations. A z-score
needs a standard deviation, so fewer than two samples is refused.

-i --mono
-d --di

Choose between a mononucleotide and dinucleotide background
model. Default is the dinucleotide model.

-v, --verbose

Produce verbose output that gives an overview of the algorithm,
intermediate results and statistics. 

--seed

Seed for the random number generator. The same seed on the same input
with the same options reproduces a run exactly. Without it the seed is
taken from the system; seeding from the clock alone gives jobs started
in the same second the same sample set. --verbose reports the seed in
use.

-t, --tstv

Use a model that has different rates for transitions and transversions.

-k, --kappa

Set the transition/transversion rate ration parameter for the
model. If not set, it is estimated from the data. If --tstv not set
this option can be ignored.

-p, --precision

Limit the deviation of the mononucleotide content in the simulated
alignments. The value given here is the maximum Euclidian distance
between the mononucleotide frequencies in the original alignment and
the simulated alignment. In other word, it is the square-root of the
sum of the squared differences between the four frequencies of As, Cs,
Ts, and Gs. For example if you set to 0.01 you will get almost exactly
the same monunucleotide content in the simulations as in the original
alignment. This makes it slower but more accurate. A value the sampler
cannot reach is given up on after 10000 rejected samples rather than
resampling forever.

-m, --num-samples-regression

Pairwise alignments of 26 different distances are simulated to
estimate the relationship between observed and genetic distances. For
each of these points the average of X samples is used, where X is the
number given here. Default is 50.

The default was 10 before 0.2.0. Raising it does not change the typical
run to run spread of the z-score, which stays near 0.2, but it makes
large excursions rarer: over 80 alignments scored twice, the share of
runs differing by more than 2 fell from 6 percent to 1 percent, for
about 12 percent more runtime. There is no further gain above 50.

-r, --nossr

Switch off site specific rates, so that every column evolves at the
same rate. The simulated alignments then reproduce the overall
divergence of the input but not its local conservation pattern.

-g, --gamma

Number of rate categories used when kappa is estimated by maximum
likelihood. Only relevant together with --tstv without --kappa.
Default is 4.

-o, --outfile

Write the results to this file instead of stdout.

-b, --print-tree

Write the inferred BIONJ tree to aln.tree.

-x, --print-rates

Write the site specific rates to rates.dat. For debugging.

-f, --flanks

In most cases you can ignore this option. In the algorithm additional
sites are used as "buffer sites" to overcome problems with saturation
of mutations that make it impossible to estimate distances. Default is
1.5 x (number of sites in original alignment). If your simulation
fails because of a negative logorithm you can raise this value.

--dna --rna

Use T or U in the output alignments. Default is --dna, i.e Ts are
printed.

--clustal --maf 

Output alignment format, either CLUSTAL W format (default) or MAF
format. Only relevant in simulation mode (--simulate).

-h --help

Print short overview of options.

-V --version

Print version information.



3.4 Output
----------

In simulation mode one or more alignmnts in CLUSTAL W or MAF format
are printed.

If --verbose is given lots of additional information is given (these
lines start with a # so that they can be filtered easily).

When calculating z-scores the results are given in one tab separated
line:

 1. Model name (either "sissiz-mono" or "sissiz-di")
 2. Name of the input file
 3. Number of sequences in the input alignment
 4. Length of the input alignment in columns
 5. Mean pairwise identity (MPI) of the input alignment
 6. Average MPI of the sampled alignments
 7. Standard deviation of the MPIs of the sampled alignments
 8. RNAalifold consensus minimum free energy (MFE) of the original alignment
 9. Average consensus MFE in the sampled alignments
10. Standard deviation of the consensus MFE in the sampled alignments
11. z-score calculated from 8., 9. and 10.

Field 6 should track field 5 closely; a large gap between them means the
simulated alignments are not reproducing the divergence of the input.


3.5 Exit status
---------------

SISSIz exits 0 on success and 1 on error, with a message on stderr. In a
genome wide screen the windows that fail are usually best skipped rather
than retried. The errors that come from the data rather than the command
line are:

  Degenerate rate matrix, the nucleotide composition of ... is too
  skewed to simulate
      Every substitution rate in the model vanishes, so there is no
      background to sample from. Typical of homopolymer or two letter
      windows.

  Could not read an alignment from ...
  Sequences are of unequal length
      The input is malformed.

  Could not reach a mononucleotide content within ...
      --precision is tighter than the sampler can reach. Raise it.

  Negative logarithm while calculating pairwise distance
      The sequences are too diverged for the fitted curve. Raising
      --flanks can help.


4. EXAMPLES
===========

SISSIz --simulate --tstv -n 1000 test.aln

  Simulate 1000 random alignments with the same average dinucleotide
  content and a transition/transversion model.

SISSIz --simulate --mono --tstv -n 1000 test.aln

  The same as above with mononucleotide model.


SISSIz --verbose --simulate --mono --tstv -n 1000 test.aln

  The same as above, but show detailed results.


SISSIz --verbose --tstv --precision 0.05 -n 1000 test.aln

  Calculate z-score with a sample size of 1000. Slowest but most
  accurate settings.


5. LITERATURE
=============

SISSIz and the new dinucleotide randomization procedure is described in:

Gesell T, Washietl S
Dinucleotide controlled null models for comparative RNA gene
prediction.
BMC Bioinformatics. 2008; 9:248


The basic idea of calculating stability z-score for multiple
alignments is described here:

Washietl S and Hofacker IL 
Consensus folding of aligned sequences as a new measure for the
detection of functional RNAs by comparative genomics.  
J Mol Biol (2004) 342:19-30.

The theoretical foundations for the simulation Model is described
here:

Gesell T and von Haeseler A 
In silico sequence evolution with site-specific interactions along
phylogenetic trees.  
Bioinformatics (2006) 22:716-22.

The RNAalifold algorithm is described here:

Hofacker IL, Fekete M, Stadler PF
Secondary structure prediction for aligned RNA sequences.
J Mol Biol (2002) 319:1059-66. 


